In article <8718@chaph.usc.edu> cchan@alcor.usc.edu writes:
>I'm playing Nethack 3.0h Patchlevel 6.  I can't seem to #offer
>any sacrifices to the Gods at the altar of my alignment, or anywhere
>else for that matter.  All I get is a "Nothing Happens" message.
>What am I doing wrong?

Only some monsters can be sacrificed to the gods.  Also, I find that if you
sacrifice one type of monster more than 3 times at one altar, it will give
you the "Nothing Happens" message.

>Is the #chat command good for anything other than talking to the
>oracle?  I have been unable to recieve any other replies than grunts
>from other monsters.

Try using the #chat command to talk to Priests that you find in altar rooms.

>What should you do with friendly monsters?  I can't chat with them,
>I can't offer them anything, and if I throw food at them they attack
>me!  Also, if I decide to kill them, does my luck go down, or does
>my God get angry at me?

Yes, any monster that when you attack says "really attack? [yn]" you
should not attack, or your luck will go down.  Friendly monsters can be
helpful if you give them the right food.  They will follow you around like
the dog or the cat.

>I ask because my God is angry at me.  The only possible bad things I
>have done is to kill a friendly giant and to pray once too soon.
>Could anybody post a list of things that will piss off the gods so
>I can avoid doing them, and a list of things that would please
>the gods?

Don't pray unless you're sure the gods will be pleased.  You can tell this by
sacrificing at an altar.  If the reply is "A four-leaf clover appears at your
feet", you can then pray.  Also, kicking an altar makes the gods angry.

>I have two uncursed scrolls of genocide that don't seem to work.
>I just keep getting this "That sort of creature does not exist in this
>world" message.  I've tried everything, d's, f's, not fantasy monsters
>such as &'s and D's.  What gives?  Is this a bug?

Nethack 3.0 has a better interface for asking questions than older versions.
When it asks you what you want to genocide it wants you to type in the
whole name.  For example, if you want to get rid of all dragons you type in
"dragons".  BTW:  Scrolls of genocide behave 3 different ways depending on
whether they are cursed, uncursed, or blessed.  A cursed one will surround
you with whatever monster you tried to genocide (this often proves deadly
when you are genociding something like rust monsters), so it is important
to make sure the scroll is not cursed.  An uncursed scroll of genocide will
allow you to get rid of all members of a certain species, e.g. red dragons,
whereas a blessed scroll allows you to kill all species, e.g. dragons.  In
terms of ranking monsters best for genocide, I would choose (best listed
first): rust monsters (evil little suckers), master liches (actually any type
of lich), gray oozes, any type of naga except golden nagas (sometimes when
you eat them you get full health back!).  Remember that many monsters are
helpful when dead and so should not be genocided.  Dragons are a good example
of this.  Each type of dragon gives you a different resistance if you eat it.
Blue dragons give cold resistance, red dragons give fire resistance, etc.
Also, unfortunately, you can not genocide the Wizard of Yendor or medusas.

>What effect does blessing/cursing have over items other than weapons
>or rings?

Cursed food items make you sick.  Cursed armor, rings, weapons, amulets,
lamps, ball and chains, etc., all cannot be removed without uncursing them.
Cursed wands have an unknown effect.  Sometimes they work, other times they
don't.  Cursed potions often make you sick or hallucinate.  All blessed items
generally work a little better than uncursed items.  Blessed food satiates
you more, blessed armor prevents you from attacks a little better, blessed
weapons do extra damage on hits, etc.  The one item that gives a much better
response is a blessed scroll of genocide (see above).

>I keep hearing about a trick that converts potions of water in holy
>water (I haven't found either yet and I'm level 14, got to dungeon level
>50 and am working my way up).  How is that done?  I have played at
>least three earlier versions of Nethack, and in all of those I acquired
>a much larger variety of items much earlier in the game.  Is this a
>bug?

You can wield a potion of water and then read a scroll of enchant weapon,
that's the only way I know how to get holy water.  A nice character to play
is the priest.  He always knows whether a potion of water is cursed,
uncursed, or blessed.

>One last question: when monsters curse at you, does that automatically
>mean an item in your inventory gets cursed?

Not always, and sometimes more than one item can get cursed.  The probability
and how many things get cursed depends on the level and type of the monster,
and a random factor also.  Any shaman monster (e.g. orc shaman) can curse
you and are probably the worst because they tend to pop up on lower dungeon
levels.  Liches are pretty good at cursing characters, especially master
liches.  Avoid them at all costs (or genocide them).  BTW:  When you get the
message "You hear a mumbled curse" that means there is a monster trying to
curse you from through a wall.  It means you are pretty close to a cursing
monster, so watch out!  A mumbled curse will not curse any of your items,
though.

